course: dictionary
lesson: 7580
title: A false reason
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pretext is a false reason that someone gives to hide their real reason. «He called on the pretext of borrowing sugar, but really he wanted to see the puppy.» Borrowing sugar was the excuse. Seeing the puppy was the real reason.
explain: Look for clues that show a hidden reason. The words «but really» in that sentence tell you the sugar was not the true story. Ask yourself: is this the real reason, or the reason someone wants me to believe?
explain: A pretext can also be an excuse used to start something, like a fight or a war. Check the meaning by swapping in the word excuse. If the sentence still makes sense, you have probably understood it.
fact: Pretext is a noun: a pretext, pretexts.
fact: «Pretext» means a false reason given to hide the real reason for doing something.
fact: Alice says the birds leave on «various pretexts», which means made-up excuses.
fact: In stories and history, a pretext shows hidden motives: someone uses it to do what they wanted all along.
fact: Scammers use pretexts such as a fake parcel or a fake prize to get your details, so always be careful online. Phrases to spot are «on the pretext of», «under the pretext that» and «a pretext for».
exercise: «He called on the pretext of borrowing sugar, but really he wanted to see the puppy.» What was his pretext? | *Borrowing sugar | To see the puppy | To mend the fence | To buy a dog
exercise: «The birds left the garden on various pretexts.» What does pretexts mean here? | Very big gardens | Lovely songs | Long journeys | *Made-up excuses
exercise: A scammer sends a message about a fake prize and asks for your bank details. Why might the scammer use a fake prize? | To make you laugh | To teach you maths | *As a pretext to get your details | To give you a real present
test: «The men came to the village under the pretext that they were selling pots, but they were spies.» What was the real reason they came? | To sing songs | To find a lost cat | To sell pots | *To spy
test: In «a pretext for a fight», what is the pretext? | *An excuse used to start a fight | A friend who breaks up fights | A prize for winning a fight | A loud shout
exam: A pupil reads «He came on the pretext of helping, but he really wanted our money.» What does the pretext hide? | *His real reason: he wanted our money | He had lost his own money | Helping was his only reason | He really was helping
